desktop-shell: a click on a subsurface activates its window

Clicking a window activates it (click_to_activate_binding). When the
click landed on a subsurface, the subsurface's view was activated, so
it got the keyboard focus and its window lost it until the focus moved
back a moment later.

Chrome (Wayland) draws its address bar suggestions in a subsurface and
closes them when its window loses the keyboard focus, so a click on a
suggestion closed the list before the click reached it: the
suggestions could not be clicked. Menus drawn as xdg_popups (the
three-dots menu) were not affected, as clicks in a popup go to the
popup grab instead of this binding.

activate_binding() now activates the view of the window (the main
surface) that holds the clicked subsurface.

diff --git a/desktop-shell/shell.c b/desktop-shell/shell.c
index b035d22..b9bbfd2 100644
--- a/desktop-shell/shell.c
+++ b/desktop-shell/shell.c
@@ -4881,6 +4881,7 @@ activate_binding(struct weston_seat *seat,
 {
 	struct weston_view *main_view;
 	struct weston_surface *main_surface;
+	struct shell_surface *shsurf;
 
 	if (!focus_view)
 		return;
@@ -4889,10 +4890,16 @@ activate_binding(struct weston_seat *seat,
 		focus_view = main_view;
 
 	main_surface = weston_surface_get_main_surface(focus_view->surface);
-	if (!get_shell_surface(main_surface))
+	shsurf = get_shell_surface(main_surface);
+	if (!shsurf)
 		return;
 
-	activate(shell, focus_view, seat, flags);
+	/* hexaOS: activate the window, not the subsurface that was clicked.
+	 * Passing the subsurface's view gave it the keyboard focus, so the
+	 * window lost focus for a moment on every click on a subsurface:
+	 * Chrome then closed its address bar suggestions (a subsurface)
+	 * before the click reached them. */
+	activate(shell, shsurf->view, seat, flags);
 }
 
 static void
